    "model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
    "seconds": 143,
    "summary": "I fixed accounting and mcp. I couldn't confirm that chatbot traffic is back.\n\n**What I found**\n- The `accounting` deployment had been scaled to 0 about 15 minutes after startup, and the Helm chart doesn't set that. Nothing was consuming the Kafka order topic. That explains `HighKafkaConsumerLag` and `KafkaConsumerGroupInactive` on the collector, which reads the topic metrics, and `NoRequestsReceived` for accounting.\n- The `agent` deployment had `MCP_ENABLED=False`. The agent never called `mcp`, which explains `NoRequestsReceived` for mcp.\n\n**What I changed**\n1. I ran `kubectl scale deploy/accounting --replicas=1`. The pod is running and processing orders from the backlog. I didn't re-check the lag alert afterwards.\n2. I ran `kubectl set env deploy/agent MCP_ENABLED=True`. The agent rolled out cleanly, and its logs and mcp's now show `POST /mcp` requests returning 200.\n\n**Still open**\n- **chatbot:** It showed no request logs in the minute after the agent change. I didn't find what sends traffic to it. It may only get requests from users or from the load generator. Check that the alert clears.\n- **Root cause:** I don't know who scaled accounting down or who set `MCP_ENABLED=False`. Both are cluster-side edits made outside Helm, so a Helm upgrade could revert them. Check the Helm values.\n- **Unrelated noise:** The collector logs errors for its `firepit` and Jaeger exporters, because those endpoints don't resolve or refuse connections. I didn't change them.",
    "timed_out": false,
    "turns": 11,
